 {
  "key": "2.7",
  "field": "solution",
  "page": 88,
  "kind": "structure",
  "note": "the claims (i), (ii) and the two cases a), b) belong to the proof of part b); they were split off as top-level lists, the cases reading like the exercise's own parts",
  "old": "  \\begin{itemize}\n    \\item[(i)] Either \\(F_{x} \\cap F_{y}=\\emptyset\\) or \\(F_{x}=F_{y}\\).\n  \\end{itemize}\n\\end{itemize}\nTo prove this we argue by contradiction: Assume that we both have\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n  \\item[(ii)] \\(F_{x} \\cap F_{y} \\neq \\emptyset\\) and \\(F_{x} \\neq F_{y}\\), e.g. \\(F_{x} \\backslash F_{y} \\neq \\emptyset\\).\n\\end{itemize}\nThen there are at most two possibilities:\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n  \\item[a)] \\(x \\in F_{x} \\cap F_{y}\\)\\\\\nThen \\(F_{x} \\cap F_{y}\\) is a set in \\(\\mathcal{F}\\) containing \\(x\\) and \\(F_{x} \\cap F_{y}\\) is strictly smaller than \\(F_{x}\\). This is impossible.\n  \\item[b)] \\(x \\in F_{x} \\backslash F_{y}\\)\\\\\nThen \\(F_{x} \\backslash F_{y}\\) is a set in \\(\\mathcal{F}\\) containing \\(x\\) and \\(F_{x} \\backslash F_{y}\\) is strictly smaller than \\(F_{x}\\). This is again impossible.\n\\end{itemize}\nHence the claim (i) is proved. Therefore there exist \\(x_{1}, \\ldots, x_{m} \\in \\Omega\\) such that\n\n\\[\nF_{x_{1}}, F_{x_{2}}, \\ldots, F_{x_{m}}\n\\]\n\nforms a partition of \\(\\Omega\\). Hence any \\(F \\in \\mathcal{F}\\) is a disjoint union of some of these \\(F_{x_{i}}\\) 's. Therefore \\(\\mathcal{F}\\) is of the form \\(\\mathcal{G}\\) described in a). \\(\\square\\)\n\n",
  "new": "  \\begin{itemize}\n    \\item[(i)] Either \\(F_{x} \\cap F_{y}=\\emptyset\\) or \\(F_{x}=F_{y}\\).\n  \\end{itemize}\nTo prove this we argue by contradiction: Assume that we both have\n  \\begin{itemize}\n    \\item[(ii)] \\(F_{x} \\cap F_{y} \\neq \\emptyset\\) and \\(F_{x} \\neq F_{y}\\), e.g. \\(F_{x} \\backslash F_{y} \\neq \\emptyset\\).\n  \\end{itemize}\nThen there are at most two possibilities:\n  \\begin{itemize}\n    \\item[a)] \\(x \\in F_{x} \\cap F_{y}\\)\\\\\nThen \\(F_{x} \\cap F_{y}\\) is a set in \\(\\mathcal{F}\\) containing \\(x\\) and \\(F_{x} \\cap F_{y}\\) is strictly smaller than \\(F_{x}\\). This is impossible.\n    \\item[b)] \\(x \\in F_{x} \\backslash F_{y}\\)\\\\\nThen \\(F_{x} \\backslash F_{y}\\) is a set in \\(\\mathcal{F}\\) containing \\(x\\) and \\(F_{x} \\backslash F_{y}\\) is strictly smaller than \\(F_{x}\\). This is again impossible.\n  \\end{itemize}\nHence the claim (i) is proved. Therefore there exist \\(x_{1}, \\ldots, x_{m} \\in \\Omega\\) such that\n\n\\[\nF_{x_{1}}, F_{x_{2}}, \\ldots, F_{x_{m}}\n\\]\n\nforms a partition of \\(\\Omega\\). Hence any \\(F \\in \\mathcal{F}\\) is a disjoint union of some of these \\(F_{x_{i}}\\) 's. Therefore \\(\\mathcal{F}\\) is of the form \\(\\mathcal{G}\\) described in a). \\(\\square\\)\n\\end{itemize}\n\n"
 },
